{"body":{"text":["Closed points at what closed it, a statement, a commit, a brief or the author's word, and may be opened again: \"Knowledge is built on its grounds, and any new ground may always exist, shifting the landscape of other pieces of knowledge\" (23/138). Of the two words offered, \"Closed is probably better. It's more precise\" than set aside, which \"can mean that it's a little bit softer than it is\" and \"tells more about the reason and might be wrong in that\", though closed is \"still ambiguous as to its reason\" (23/138); a blank reader offered both (23/137)."]},"id":"1M89J53JV0M7000","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A **topic** is a short name, holding statements: \"topics need short names because topics naturally are addressable\" (23/116). A statement may stand in several topics, the session's answer (23/111); topics may hold topics, as he once thought, \"you can structure a group and there's a hierarchy in and of itself\" (23/86), and took their composing: \"Yeah, why not?\" (23/112). A topic keeps its spans, the stretches of sessions where it was spoken of, each written between(session, from, to) (23/79, 23/80). Its spans are read from its statements' grounds, written by hand only where talk ran with nothing stated (23/129). A topic with nothing in Knowledge yet is, as the session put it, a debt in plain sight (23/91). Whether a topic is a brief is open (23/104 to 23/108)."]},"id":"1M89J53JV0M7001","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}